--TEST--
A JSON body hydrated into a `T` whose text fields are unqualified is refused at the call that decodes, for the written class and for the classes its fields name `rule:security/derived-codec-qualifiers`, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
--HEADERS--
Content-Type: application/json
--POST_RAW--
{"title":"ada","author":{"name":"ada"},"tags":["one"]}
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/derived-codec-qualifiers`: a derived decoder assigns a peer's
// octets straight into the declared property types, so a field that receives
// them is the one that declares them. The codec cannot ask that question — the
// same class is legitimate over a document the program built itself — so it is
// asked at the call that decodes, and reported at the declaration, which is
// where the `tainted` is written.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Author {
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(string $name) {
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Post {
    // The accepted side of the bound, named beside the refused ones: `tainted`
    // on the declaration is the whole of what the rule asks for, and no issue
    // below is about this property.
    public tainted string $title;
    // A field naming another deriving class is filled out of the same document,
    // so its own text fields receive the same octets and answer for them the
    // same way.
    public Author $author;
    // A list decodes element by element into the declared element type, so a
    // list of text is where the qualifier is written for its elements.
    public array<string> $tags;
    // Only `string` and `bytes` carry a qualifier at all, so a number reaches
    // none of this — the refusal is about text and says so by leaving this one
    // alone.
    public int $reads;

    public function constructor(
        tainted string $title,
        Author $author,
        array<string> $tags,
        int $reads,
    ) {
        $this->title = $title;
        $this->author = $author;
        $this->tags = $tags;
        $this->reads = $reads;
    }
}

// One call, and every property the document reaches is answered for it: the
// site is what makes the octets a peer's, and the whole codec is what receives
// them.
Post $post = Core\Request::jsonAs<Post>();
echo $post->title, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0810]: `Author::$name` is declared `string`, and `Core\Request::jsonAs` answers a peer's octets
%A
error[E0810]: `Post::$tags` is declared `array<string>`, and `Core\Request::jsonAs` answers a peer's octets
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
